46
00:06:04,220 --> 00:06:05,640
But very briefly, what-

47
00:06:06,592 --> 00:06:09,292
... is the problem of ML? What are we trying to do in machine learning?

48
00:06:09,832 --> 00:06:13,472
And I think we can consider this to be solving this one equation,

49
00:06:14,132 --> 00:06:17,112
y is f of x, where we don't know what f is.

50
00:06:18,172 --> 00:06:22,152
So we have some inputs, and we want to find out what's the output meant

51
00:06:22,192 --> 00:06:24,172
to be, and that varies by problem.

52
00:06:25,052 --> 00:06:28,872
And we approximate the function by throwing lots and lots

53
00:06:28,992 --> 00:06:32,852
of examples at a model of some sort, and we

54
00:06:32,912 --> 00:06:36,572
end up with an approximation for f that lets us make predictions on

55
00:06:36,652 --> 00:06:40,632
further unknown y's. And I'll show you an example so this makes

56
00:06:40,652 --> 00:06:43,432
more sense maybe. So here's an example from my own work.

57
00:06:43,912 --> 00:06:45,672
We have a picture of a herbarium sheet.

58
00:06:46,032 --> 00:06:49,552
It has this nice specimen on it, and we want to know, are there buds in this

59
00:06:49,592 --> 00:06:51,452
picture? Are there flowers? Are there fruits?

60
00:06:51,892 --> 00:06:55,672
And you can imagine there's some function, perhaps a well-defined function, which

61
00:06:55,692 --> 00:06:58,952
can give us this answer from this image, but we don't know that function.

62
00:06:59,312 --> 00:07:02,492
So we have a neural network which tries to estimate or

63
00:07:02,512 --> 00:07:05,932
approximate the function to give us a good prediction

64
00:07:06,012 --> 00:07:08,352
for are there buds, are there flowers, are there fruits.

65
00:07:09,472 --> 00:07:12,172
And this is maybe an example of training data.

66
00:07:12,492 --> 00:07:16,092
You have lots and lots of images in this case, and each of them is annotated

67
00:07:16,412 --> 00:07:19,712
saying if there's fruit, if there's buds, if there's flowers, and we throw this

68
00:07:19,792 --> 00:07:21,292
into the neural network. We train.

69
00:07:21,632 --> 00:07:25,532
We hopefully do something a little bit clever in the background, and eventually, we

70
00:07:25,572 --> 00:07:29,532
get our answer like this. So it has predicted buds, flowers, fruits.

71
00:07:29,792 --> 00:07:31,032
It's more or less right.

72
00:07:32,112 --> 00:07:34,232
So hopefully, that's how it works.

73
00:07:34,732 --> 00:07:37,632
But I think one thing we'll be talking about a lot in this session, certainly we

74
00:07:37,672 --> 00:07:40,932
talked about it last year in Tadwigg, and is

75
00:07:40,972 --> 00:07:44,932
maybe a big topic of discussion in the general discussion too,

76
00:07:45,452 --> 00:07:46,592
are large language models.

77
00:07:47,992 --> 00:07:49,532
So very briefly, large language models.

78
00:07:49,832 --> 00:07:53,652
These are, I think I would like to call it an advance in natural language

79
00:07:53,692 --> 00:07:54,232
processing,

80
00:07:55,152 --> 00:07:58,872
based on transformers. And I think it's possible to argue that they are

81
00:07:58,952 --> 00:08:02,272
qualitatively different from previous machine learning

82
00:08:03,412 --> 00:08:07,252
models in that they speak to us, which I think is something

83
00:08:07,272 --> 00:08:11,052
which a lot of people find very striking, very surprising, that you are not just

84
00:08:11,092 --> 00:08:14,792
looking at numbers or XML files, but rather you're getting natural language

85
00:08:14,831 --> 00:08:16,391
responses to your natural language queries.

86
00:08:17,632 --> 00:08:19,252
I think their performance is very hard to measure.

87
00:08:19,412 --> 00:08:23,232
It's easy to say intuitively, is it doing a good job when

88
00:08:23,372 --> 00:08:26,392
you're speaking with one, but hard to measure in an objective way.

89
00:08:27,172 --> 00:08:30,132
And of course, they require huge amounts of training data. They're very expensive.

90
00:08:30,552 --> 00:08:33,252
This is a problem that we're all aware of, I think.

91
00:08:34,672 --> 00:08:35,712
Oh, a blank slide.

92
00:08:38,972 --> 00:08:39,352
Oh, okay.

93
00:08:41,952 --> 00:08:45,272
Yeah, so these large language models, basically what they do is predict the next

94
00:08:45,312 --> 00:08:47,752
word, and they've been trained on a huge corpus.

95
00:08:48,292 --> 00:08:51,532
People say all human knowledge, that's an exaggeration, but quite a lot of

96
00:08:51,932 --> 00:08:55,852
written human text at least. And they also solve this problem,

97
00:08:56,012 --> 00:08:56,692
y is f of x.

98
00:08:58,372 --> 00:09:02,032
And here, for example, of a standard sentence that you would find in the training

99
00:09:02,092 --> 00:09:04,592
corpus, "It was the day my grandmother exploded."

100
00:09:06,132 --> 00:09:09,892
And you might start with it as an input, "It was the day my grandmother,"

101
00:09:10,332 --> 00:09:14,172
and you ask what's the next word or what's the next token in this

102
00:09:14,232 --> 00:09:18,152
sentence. And of course, here, as we all know, it's exploded, followed by a

103
00:09:18,192 --> 00:09:20,772
full stop, followed by an end of sentence marker.

104
00:09:21,272 --> 00:09:25,072
And so if the model guessed these, it would be marked correct because that's what's

105
00:09:25,132 --> 00:09:29,112
in the training data. But of course, you can also choose different x,

106
00:09:29,632 --> 00:09:33,172
different inputs, and then based on the training data,

107
00:09:33,392 --> 00:09:36,992
which is many sentences like this, where you cover up part of the

108
00:09:37,032 --> 00:09:39,492
sentence, you have the model guess, you show it the answer,

109
00:09:40,552 --> 00:09:43,152
it can find some plausible output.

110
00:09:45,212 --> 00:09:47,572
And this is what we see in the chat box as well.

111
00:09:48,052 --> 00:09:50,892
You get a system prompt, which is just a bunch of words at the start.

112
00:09:51,052 --> 00:09:54,692
You get the user prompt, which is you saying, "Hey, what's the capital of

113
00:09:54,752 --> 00:09:55,052
Norway?"

114
00:09:56,572 --> 00:10:00,372
You get the conversation so far, if there is one previously, and the

115
00:10:00,692 --> 00:10:04,652
large language model looks at its own input as well when predicting the next word.

116
00:10:04,892 --> 00:10:08,752
So what's the capital of Norway? Hopefully, it says Oslo, and then it knows

117
00:10:08,792 --> 00:10:11,392
this in the conversation. That's what we call the context window.

118
00:10:11,672 --> 00:10:15,652
So it will predict the next word based on all of this previous context.

119
00:10:17,232 --> 00:10:18,432
And that's it. That's all they do.

120
00:10:19,672 --> 00:10:22,732
It's really impressive, I think. It's really surprising that we get this very

121
00:10:22,852 --> 00:10:26,652
natural-feeling conversation from something which conceptually at least is very

122
00:10:26,691 --> 00:10:30,632
simple. Of course, there's a lot of exciting, interesting maths in

123
00:10:30,672 --> 00:10:32,212
the background. How do you do the weighting?

124
00:10:33,112 --> 00:10:36,712
There's a bunch of bolt-ons where you can have them call Python functions or

125
00:10:36,732 --> 00:10:40,132
whatever, but in essence, that's what a large language model does and what it is.

126
00:10:40,772 --> 00:10:44,592
And if you have a reasoning model, that means it prompts itself again and

127
00:10:44,632 --> 00:10:48,492
again with some additional instructions like this, but there's

128
00:10:48,552 --> 00:10:52,212
no actual reasoning in the sense that we have it, I would say.

129
00:10:53,492 --> 00:10:56,472
Maybe that's a philosophical question, but I feel comfortable making that claim.

130
00:10:58,752 --> 00:11:02,172
And the one thing I wanted to finish on this topic is

131
00:11:02,852 --> 00:11:06,312
what we call hallucinations, which is basically where we say, "It's made something

132
00:11:06,392 --> 00:11:07,372
