1377
01:23:50,138 --> 01:23:53,778
A little bit of a background, although maybe it's not that needed at this point.

1378
01:23:54,258 --> 01:23:57,918
Camera trapping has been steadily gaining popularity over many years now,

1379
01:23:58,338 --> 01:23:59,798
and this is due to several reasons.

1380
01:24:00,638 --> 01:24:02,898
Hardware is becoming cheaper and more accessible.

1381
01:24:03,278 --> 01:24:07,098
There are more methods developed for monitoring and researching animals using

1382
01:24:07,138 --> 01:24:10,998
camera traps as well as specifically in the last couple of years,

1383
01:24:11,378 --> 01:24:15,178
a couple of very cool AI models have been released to streamline this

1384
01:24:15,218 --> 01:24:16,238
work as well.

1385
01:24:17,078 --> 01:24:19,438
Camera trapping generates very big datasets,

1386
01:24:20,558 --> 01:24:24,198
and those cool models are around, but still, many institutions rely on manual

1387
01:24:24,298 --> 01:24:28,058
analysis of this data. And manual analysis means that it can

1388
01:24:28,098 --> 01:24:31,838
take a year or longer between when the picture is taken and when the

1389
01:24:31,858 --> 01:24:33,758
researchers find out what is in this picture.

1390
01:24:34,238 --> 01:24:37,218
So this branch of research really needs automation.

1391
01:24:38,338 --> 01:24:40,518
So how does Trapper fit into this picture?

1392
01:24:41,278 --> 01:24:45,018
Trapper is an open-source data infrastructure for managing data in camera

1393
01:24:45,058 --> 01:24:48,838
trapping projects. And when I say data infrastructure, I mean it's not

1394
01:24:48,938 --> 01:24:52,378
just a single app that you put your photos into and get some kind of AI results.

1395
01:24:52,818 --> 01:24:56,638
It's a whole suite of tools for different aspects of managing

1396
01:24:56,678 --> 01:24:59,838
these projects for different users with different skills and needs.

1397
01:25:01,582 --> 01:25:03,582
Trapper works with videos as well as photos.

1398
01:25:04,062 --> 01:25:07,802
This year, Trapper is turning 10, and for most of this time it only worked with

1399
01:25:07,822 --> 01:25:08,242
photos.

1400
01:25:09,162 --> 01:25:11,242
But we are now working on a big update.

1401
01:25:11,362 --> 01:25:15,122
That's what Trapper version two is, and it's capable of analyzing

1402
01:25:15,162 --> 01:25:19,002
videos as well. It is still in the final testing phase, but we're planning an

1403
01:25:19,062 --> 01:25:20,802
official release before the end of this year.

1404
01:25:22,762 --> 01:25:25,402
Trapper enables both AI and human annotation.

1405
01:25:25,562 --> 01:25:29,282
So on the one hand, it gives you the benefit of automation, but on the other hand,

1406
01:25:29,362 --> 01:25:32,642
you still have human in the loop. You still have expert supervision on these

1407
01:25:32,682 --> 01:25:33,162
results.

1408
01:25:34,602 --> 01:25:38,222
Trapper can use many different AI models in a fully customizable pipeline.

1409
01:25:39,742 --> 01:25:43,502
It's scalable, it's suitable for running projects of different sizes, small up

1410
01:25:43,562 --> 01:25:45,562
to big and very big as well.

1411
01:25:46,702 --> 01:25:49,582
And Trapper is compatible with Camtrap DP data standard.

1412
01:25:50,682 --> 01:25:53,702
So let's now zoom in on a couple of these qualities.

1413
