03:18:35,870 --> 03:18:39,830
these statements that are basically three statements or more statements all in

2516
03:18:39,950 --> 03:18:43,910
one. So the brown fur, white legs, short tail, this is not

2517
03:18:43,970 --> 03:18:47,950
one statement. These are three things, and we want it to be modular and tease

2518
03:18:47,970 --> 03:18:51,410
that apart, so make three statements out of this example.

2519
03:18:53,550 --> 03:18:57,390
Another thing that we have to tell it to do is try to be as

2520
03:18:57,430 --> 03:18:58,690
precise as possible.

2521
03:18:59,670 --> 03:19:03,030
A mouse may have in a description a

2522
03:19:03,330 --> 03:19:04,610
relatively long tail,

2523
03:19:05,470 --> 03:19:08,670
which is very useful if you know all of your mice.

2524
03:19:09,030 --> 03:19:13,010
But if you're just faced with one specimen of mouse, how long can

2525
03:19:13,030 --> 03:19:13,310
it be?

2526
03:19:14,990 --> 03:19:18,230
So we tell it to go to the text and try to find the real numbers.

2527
03:19:18,290 --> 03:19:19,190
They're often there,

2528
03:19:20,030 --> 03:19:23,690
and in such cases, we tell it to go back to the real

2529
03:19:23,750 --> 03:19:24,210
sources

2530
03:19:25,870 --> 03:19:27,170
and work in that precise manner.

2531
03:19:28,470 --> 03:19:28,690
So

2532
03:19:29,709 --> 03:19:30,130
with this,

2533
03:19:31,110 --> 03:19:34,889
per book, we have a list of statements of things it found out about these

2534
03:19:34,970 --> 03:19:38,770
species. What we then do is we have an audit run by a

2535
03:19:38,850 --> 03:19:39,590
separate agent.

2536
03:19:41,310 --> 03:19:45,050
So we spin up a brand new agent who's never seen the key before,

2537
03:19:45,290 --> 03:19:48,830
never seen the statements, and it has nothing to defend.

2538
03:19:49,270 --> 03:19:52,650
This works way better than getting the original agent

2539
03:19:52,730 --> 03:19:56,670
to correct its own homework because it's going to defend the choices made along

2540
03:19:56,710 --> 03:20:00,290
the way, and it works a lot better to

2541
03:20:02,470 --> 03:20:04,370
have a naive agent doing this.

2542
03:20:06,370 --> 03:20:10,210
Then we come to the fun step, where we take all of these keys and we start merging

2543
03:20:10,250 --> 03:20:14,030
them. And a lot of this is quite easy. You find the same characteristics.

2544
03:20:14,130 --> 03:20:17,290
You combine the questions. But it has to do some

2545
03:20:17,990 --> 03:20:21,050
more interesting stuff as well. It has to look at the actual content.

2546
03:20:21,670 --> 03:20:25,330
Something like pale yellow and whitish yellow is not something you're going to be

2547
03:20:25,510 --> 03:20:28,410
able to tell apart in the field. They're basically the same thing.

2548
03:20:28,770 --> 03:20:31,170
So it has to know that. It has to combine them.

2549
03:20:32,690 --> 03:20:35,730
And every such choice it makes, we have a document.

2550
03:20:35,810 --> 03:20:39,790
So we know that it has made the decision that these are the same thing,

2551
03:20:39,990 --> 03:20:42,390
and we or an expert can look at that.

2552
03:20:43,690 --> 03:20:45,350
It also moves things up the taxon tree.

2553
03:20:45,410 --> 03:20:49,370
If a whole taxon, a whole genus shares a characteristic, it's moved

2554
03:20:49,450 --> 03:20:50,850
to the genus level.

2555
03:20:52,710 --> 03:20:56,650
By the way, we can also have it translate at this stage if we want, not just a

2556
03:20:56,730 --> 03:21:00,110
Norwegian key, but we want it in English as well. We can tell it to do that.

2557
03:21:00,250 --> 03:21:03,970
We can give it some source material in English, like a good

2558
03:21:04,030 --> 03:21:07,270
Wikipedia article with the right terminology. It can use that.

2559
03:21:07,310 --> 03:21:08,190
That works really well.

2560
03:21:11,890 --> 03:21:15,770
Then finally, we have a grounding step where we take another

2561
03:21:15,830 --> 03:21:19,730
fresh agent, and we tell it to go through all of the statements it now has in

2562
03:21:19,770 --> 03:21:21,490
the key and see if it can

2563
03:21:22,510 --> 03:21:26,390
verify that all of this is somewhere within the original material.

2564
03:21:26,830 --> 03:21:28,550
We don't want it to hallucinate anything.

2565
03:21:30,550 --> 03:21:33,210
This works really well.

2566
03:21:34,390 --> 03:21:36,950
In terms of costs, we were asked to mention this.

2567
03:21:37,430 --> 03:21:39,250
Expert, really expensive.

2568
03:21:40,090 --> 03:21:42,010
If you have a single key, you can do this for

2569
03:21:43,030 --> 03:21:44,670
10 bucks within an hour.

2570
03:21:46,110 --> 03:21:48,830
If you have six books, it takes a couple of hours.

2571
03:21:50,270 --> 03:21:54,189
I estimate it's within the range of up to $30, which is,

2572
03:21:54,710 --> 03:21:56,750
compared to an expert, a no-brainer.

2573
03:21:57,830 --> 03:21:59,610
It does not, however, give you a perfect key.

2574
03:21:59,790 --> 03:22:00,730
The perfect key has

2575
03:22:01,690 --> 03:22:02,650
good illustrations.

2576
03:22:04,410 --> 03:22:07,450
We've tried with generative AI. It will fail.

2577
03:22:07,670 --> 03:22:09,990
It will look okay,

2578
03:22:11,490 --> 03:22:15,370
but it won't be perfect. Like the yellow-necked mouse, the yellow

2579
03:22:15,430 --> 03:22:17,350
neck is in the wrong place. So

2580
03:22:19,250 --> 03:22:23,070
we don't have a solution for this yet, and that is

2581
03:22:23,130 --> 03:22:26,970
why we want to collaborate with experts that will make these good illustrations.

2582
03:22:28,550 --> 03:22:32,030
And that's a general thing. This is not a ready-made key.

2583
03:22:32,190 --> 03:22:35,390
It's not perfect, but this is something that we can give to a

2584
03:22:35,470 --> 03:22:38,890
taxonomist to go through and to

2585
03:22:38,950 --> 03:22:42,770
verify. So their job becomes, instead of transcribing and creating

2586
03:22:42,830 --> 03:22:44,290
this key, it becomes to

2587
03:22:45,170 --> 03:22:48,930
be a reviewer, which is, we think, a very big step forward.

2588
03:22:49,590 --> 03:22:53,490
And these keys are quite good and quite usable in our experience.

2589
03:22:53,650 --> 03:22:53,830
So

2590
03:22:55,370 --> 03:22:58,930
just a short plug, if you want to hear more about the actual key formats, how we

