4942
07:50:55,232 --> 07:50:59,212
context to clearly identify which species is meant, and this is

4943
07:50:59,232 --> 07:51:01,132
not trivial for computers.

4944
07:51:03,292 --> 07:51:07,252
The number of synonyms is huge. Here we see a small selection

4945
07:51:07,311 --> 07:51:10,992
of synonyms for this one species, and in many

4946
07:51:11,052 --> 07:51:14,652
taxonomic databases, synonyms outnumber

4947
07:51:14,732 --> 07:51:15,652
accepted names.

4948
07:51:17,032 --> 07:51:20,032
As an example, we can take the plant list, which had

4949
07:51:20,832 --> 07:51:22,432
44% synonyms

4950
07:51:23,832 --> 07:51:26,752
with only 33% of accepted names.

4951
07:51:29,152 --> 07:51:31,572
But not only names change over time.

4952
07:51:32,552 --> 07:51:36,032
With many well-known names being reduced to synonyms,

4953
07:51:36,952 --> 07:51:40,752
the taxonomic concepts behind the names may also

4954
07:51:40,812 --> 07:51:42,772
change, and this is often overlooked.

4955
07:51:43,172 --> 07:51:46,712
The same binomial name may refer to different taxonomic

4956
07:51:46,772 --> 07:51:50,712
concepts, which may be more or less congruent, but often they

4957
07:51:50,732 --> 07:51:53,852
are only partly overlapping. Take the example of the

4958
07:51:54,252 --> 07:51:58,012
European oak, Quercus robur. This name was coined by

4959
07:51:58,052 --> 07:51:58,652
Linnaeus,

4960
07:51:59,912 --> 07:52:03,292
but it had a very different circumscription than Quercus

4961
07:52:03,352 --> 07:52:06,872
robur L. in the sense of modern authors.

4962
07:52:07,252 --> 07:52:10,892
Nevertheless, modern authors claim it to be a Linnaean

4963
07:52:10,952 --> 07:52:11,332
name.

4964
07:52:13,072 --> 07:52:16,792
The phenomenon of shifting taxonomic concepts must be taken into

4965
07:52:16,852 --> 07:52:20,132
account when extracting data from legacy literature,

4966
07:52:20,712 --> 07:52:23,412
which spans centuries of taxonomic change.

4967
07:52:24,332 --> 07:52:28,032
And this is far more challenging than mere synonymy

4968
07:52:28,092 --> 07:52:28,552
problems.

4969
07:52:31,252 --> 07:52:35,172
So in connection with synonymy and homonymy, there are some domain-specific

4970
07:52:35,212 --> 07:52:39,012
characteristics, especially in taxonomic texts,

4971
07:52:39,092 --> 07:52:40,252
which require special

4972
07:52:41,092 --> 07:52:45,072
consideration. Latin modifiers such as sensu,

4973
07:52:45,552 --> 07:52:49,392
auctorum, non, nec, propter, and so on, often

4974
07:52:49,452 --> 07:52:53,392
show that identical binomial names may refer to different

4975
07:52:53,452 --> 07:52:53,932
taxa.

4976
07:52:54,792 --> 07:52:58,052
Same example here, Quercus robur sensu

4977
07:52:58,132 --> 07:53:02,112
lato often means Linnaeus' broad concept of

4978
07:53:02,152 --> 07:53:06,092
Quercus robur, while Quercus robur sensu stricto

4979
07:53:06,152 --> 07:53:09,712
could mean the modern narrow concept of this species.

4980
07:53:12,432 --> 07:53:15,052
Another aspect is homonymy, of course.

4981
07:53:15,252 --> 07:53:18,912
The genus name Liparis is not only a botanical name, but

4982
07:53:19,012 --> 07:53:21,852
also a genus of fish is called Liparis.

4983
07:53:22,512 --> 07:53:25,592
It's a toponym for a lake in Canada and so on.

4984
07:53:26,432 --> 07:53:30,172
A more prominent example of homonymy is Paris, which is a

4985
07:53:30,232 --> 07:53:33,792
genus of plant, a city in Texas, a city in France, and so on.

4986
07:53:35,932 --> 07:53:39,692
We see that phenomena such as synonymy, homonymy, and shifting

4987
07:53:39,752 --> 07:53:43,512
taxon concepts create ambiguity in legacy

4988
07:53:43,552 --> 07:53:47,252
literature. What could be the steps to find solutions?

4989
07:53:47,812 --> 07:53:49,752
First thing is to develop

4990
07:53:50,672 --> 07:53:54,472
even more comprehensive taxonomic backbones to resolve synonymy.

4991
07:53:56,052 --> 07:53:59,352
We need then to improve taxonomic entity recognition tools.

4992
07:54:00,092 --> 07:54:03,992
We need to incorporate broader context for automatic

4993
07:54:04,052 --> 07:54:07,172
taxonomic name disambiguation.

4994
07:54:08,092 --> 07:54:12,072
We should enable temporal disambiguation of taxon concepts, which

4995
07:54:12,112 --> 07:54:16,072
could mean to capture historical data on the evolution of

4996
07:54:16,132 --> 07:54:19,652
concepts, and we should implement or expand, of course,

4997
07:54:19,952 --> 07:54:23,692
standards like the Taxon Concept Standard, and

4998
07:54:24,072 --> 07:54:26,132
CATWIC, of course, has an important role here.

4999
07:54:28,112 --> 07:54:31,172
Next aspect is coreference

5000
07:54:31,752 --> 07:54:35,052
resolution. In linguistics, coreferences

5001
07:54:35,152 --> 07:54:38,692
occur when two or more expressions refer to the same

5002
07:54:38,792 --> 07:54:40,532
object. In this example,

5003
07:54:41,852 --> 07:54:45,592
the expressions Coeloglossum viride and Coeloglossum

5004
07:54:46,652 --> 07:54:50,412
are shown, and it is clear for human readers that in the

5005
07:54:50,452 --> 07:54:53,492
latter case, the author has just dispensed with the longer

5006
07:54:53,552 --> 07:54:56,712
designation out of economy, but is nevertheless

5007
07:54:57,252 --> 07:55:00,192
referring to the same species, Coeloglossum viride.

5008
07:55:00,932 --> 07:55:04,452
And there are many other types of coreferences which are not easy for

5009
07:55:04,492 --> 07:55:05,632
computers to resolve.

5010
07:55:07,292 --> 07:55:11,192
So for NLP tools, coreferences continue to be a

5011
07:55:11,312 --> 07:55:12,752
significant challenge.

5012
07:55:15,812 --> 07:55:19,032
Coreferences are common and take many forms, and they may

5013
07:55:19,992 --> 07:55:22,412
several sentences or several paragraphs.

5014
07:55:23,492 --> 07:55:27,112
And taxon-related coreferences include domain-specific

5015
07:55:27,172 --> 07:55:28,172
forms. For example,

5016
07:55:29,012 --> 07:55:32,992
as mentioned, C. viride can mean the same as Coeloglossum

5017
07:55:33,052 --> 07:55:33,452
viride,

5018
07:55:34,332 --> 07:55:37,872
or a mere Coeloglossum can mean the same as Coeloglossum

5019
07:55:37,932 --> 07:55:41,732
viride, or a mere var. nanum could

5020
07:55:41,772 --> 07:55:44,232
mean Coeloglossum viride var. nanum.

5021
07:55:45,212 --> 07:55:48,412
How to cope with coreferences like this?

5022
07:55:49,592 --> 07:55:52,932
First, we need to apply state-of-the-art NLP for

5023
07:55:53,032 --> 07:55:56,452
general coreference resolutions, including

5024
07:55:56,512 --> 07:56:00,232
transformer models, for example, but it will also be

5025
07:56:00,292 --> 07:56:03,132
important to integrate domain-specific tools

5026
07:56:04,132 --> 07:56:06,772
such as nomenclatural ontologies.

5027
07:56:08,392 --> 07:56:11,132
Last aspect here is mixture of languages.

5028
07:56:12,612 --> 07:56:15,992
Mixture of languages often occurs in biodiversity

5029
07:56:16,052 --> 07:56:19,232
documents. In this example, three languages are mixed.

5030
07:56:20,012 --> 07:56:23,532
The paper is in German. There's a Latin diagnosis and also a

5031
07:56:24,412 --> 07:56:25,852
specimen citation in English.

5032
07:56:27,412 --> 07:56:30,892
In fact, many tools for natural language processing

5033
07:56:30,952 --> 07:56:34,692
require prior specification of the language of a document.

5034
07:56:35,052 --> 07:56:37,092
Such tools will give poor result.

5035
07:56:38,252 --> 07:56:41,912
So it will be necessary to select tools which are able to

5036
07:56:41,972 --> 07:56:45,752
detect language on a per sentence or per paragraph

5037
07:56:45,812 --> 07:56:49,332
basis. And for low-resource languages like Latin,

5038
07:56:49,652 --> 07:56:51,512
we also need special solutions.

5039
07:56:52,892 --> 07:56:56,832
So legacy literature holds invaluable biodiversity

5040
07:56:56,952 --> 07:57:00,612
data waiting to be unlocked. But here's the challenge:

5041
07:57:01,012 --> 07:57:04,872
we can't access it effectively yet due to OCR errors,

5042
07:57:04,912 --> 07:57:07,432
synonyms, shifting concepts and so on.

5043
07:57:07,792 --> 07:57:11,632
So how do we move forward? I think the answer lies in a

5044
07:57:11,692 --> 07:57:15,452
coordinated approach. First, we need technical solutions.

5045
07:57:16,112 --> 07:57:19,672
We must advance OCR precision, leverage cutting-edge

5046
07:57:19,792 --> 07:57:23,712
NLP, including domain-specific ontologies, and these

5047
07:57:23,772 --> 07:57:27,072
tools are the foundation for cleaning and structuring raw data.

5048
07:57:27,612 --> 07:57:30,692
Second, we need taxonomic rigor. We need to further

5049
07:57:30,892 --> 07:57:34,632
strengthen taxonomic backbones, adopt universal

5050
07:57:34,712 --> 07:57:37,992
standards, enable temporal disambiguation.

5051
07:57:38,092 --> 07:57:40,552
After all, a name in 1850

5052
07:57:41,772 --> 07:57:44,992
may not mean the same today. Our systems must account for that.

5053
07:57:45,492 --> 07:57:47,772
So I think this is

5054
07:57:48,592 --> 07:57:52,412
a hard way, but the reward would be immense because we would be

5055
07:57:52,532 --> 07:57:53,752
able to mobilize

5056
07:57:54,572 --> 07:57:56,592
much valuable data with this. Thank you.

