5290
08:11:46,988 --> 08:11:48,228
So what can we do about that?

5291
08:11:51,128 --> 08:11:52,848
There are various ways to explore search results.

5292
08:11:52,908 --> 08:11:55,328
This will be another little live demo to have a look at.

5293
08:11:55,868 --> 08:11:59,648
I've always been obsessed with the idea about how names change over time in BHL.

5294
08:12:00,708 --> 08:12:04,468
What you can do here is put in a name or a pair of names

5295
08:12:04,788 --> 08:12:06,488
and see how they've changed over time.

5296
08:12:07,368 --> 08:12:10,808
If you're a fan of Wikipedia, there's a famous, famous

5297
08:12:10,948 --> 08:12:13,148
example of-- this is the sperm whale.

5298
08:12:13,388 --> 08:12:16,908
There are two Linnaean names for the sperm whale, and there's a

5299
08:12:17,028 --> 08:12:18,628
huge, very foul-mouthed

5300
08:12:19,588 --> 08:12:22,188
debate on Wikipedia about which name to use for the page.

5301
08:12:22,768 --> 08:12:26,488
And you can go and do a quick search and see how names have changed over

5302
08:12:26,548 --> 08:12:30,448
time. Is that useful? Is that something BHL should do? This is live.

5303
08:12:30,508 --> 08:12:31,428
You can have a play with it.

5304
08:12:33,388 --> 08:12:37,288
We've heard a lot about OCR. I'm going to be slightly

5305
08:12:37,328 --> 08:12:38,328
bold about OCR.

5306
08:12:39,768 --> 08:12:42,868
So the OCR landscape has changed dramatically in the last few years.

5307
08:12:43,108 --> 08:12:45,568
There are now amazing LLMs. This is my favorite.

5308
08:12:45,588 --> 08:12:48,488
This is Datalab, which I think is a company in Boston.

5309
08:12:49,348 --> 08:12:53,248
Harris talked earlier about particularly Mistral, which is the OCR

5310
08:12:53,308 --> 08:12:55,128
engine the museum is using in London.

5311
08:12:56,188 --> 08:12:57,867
It is extraordinary what these things can do.

5312
08:12:58,188 --> 08:13:00,008
And to give you a sense of this,

5313
08:13:01,867 --> 08:13:05,748
so on the left is the OCR for this page in BHL today.

5314
08:13:06,288 --> 08:13:09,608
On the right is essentially completely unmodified

5315
08:13:09,728 --> 08:13:12,728
OCR for the same article that's come out of Datalab.

5316
08:13:13,228 --> 08:13:17,168
That is almost as good as what you'll see in one of Lubo's pencil journals.

5317
08:13:17,628 --> 08:13:21,308
I mean, it's pulled out tables, figures, figure captions, the whole lot.

5318
08:13:22,088 --> 08:13:25,928
To a first approximation, OCR is solved, and obviously not

5319
08:13:25,968 --> 08:13:28,128
entirely, but this is extraordinary.

5320
08:13:28,508 --> 08:13:30,628
So the only barrier now is cost.

5321
08:13:34,788 --> 08:13:38,688
Geography. This is an area that has been somewhat is a bit neglected,

5322
08:13:38,768 --> 08:13:42,728
I guess, in the BHL interface. I want to show you a few ways we can play with that.

5323
08:13:44,528 --> 08:13:48,048
Just to give you a sense of what's in there, this is a tiny subset of BHL data.

